<a id="set-up-the-host"></a>
<h2>Set up the Host</h2>

<p>
  This process is fairly straightforward and just involves a couple configuration tweaks and package installations. For
  packages, we will install <code>pkg install doas linux_base-rl9 linux-rl9-vulkan linux-steam-utils pulseaudio</code>.
  Some of these may pull each other in or already come with your window manager.
</p>

<p>
  Following this, a few config tweaks are needed. First, <code>sysrc linux_enable=yes</code> to enable the Linuxulator.
  To make this take effect immediately, you can do <code>kldload linux.so; kldload linux64.so</code>.
</p>

<p>
  Next, we will add some lines to <code>/etc/sysctl.conf</code>:

  <pre>
security.bsd.unprivileged_chroot=1
compat.linux.default_openfiles=65535</pre>

  Once these are added, run <code>sysctl -f /etc/sysctl.conf</code> for them to take effect immediately.
</p>

<a id="set-up-the-jail"></a>
<h2>Set up the Jail</h2>

<a id="custom-configuration"></a>
<h2>Custom Configuration</h2>

<a id="notes"></a>
<h3>Notes:</h3>

<p>
  For best results, make sure your <code>pkg(8)</code> repos are pointing at <code>current</code> instead of
  <code>quarterly</code>.
</p>

<p>
  <code>127.0.0.1</code> should be in your jail's address list, and your jail's primary IP should not be shared with any
  other jail.
</p>
<p>
  A FreeBSD jail without <code>127.0.0.1</code> in its address list has its loopback traffic rewritten to use the jail's
  primary address. Steam rejects this traffic because it appears to come from a non-loopback address. If you are getting
  persistent <code>Unexpected transport error</code> messages, this is likely the cause. FreeBSD only allows jails to
  share a single address from one family, so if your jail's primary IP is already being shared, you cannot share the
  loopback address.
</p>

